{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "b7e3a1c2-5f4d-4e8a-9c6b-2d1f0a3e5b7c",
    "name": "AI Gateway Enforcement — mcp-todos-server",
    "description": "Demo collection for the session-level AI Gateway enforcement gate on /mcp (mcp-todos-server/app/gateway_gate.py).\n\n**The story it tells:**\n1. **Setup** — mint an enforcement JWT straight from PingOne app #7 (the same credential the Privilege gateway injects into `Authorization` in production — minting it directly proves what the MCP server requires, with no gateway needed). Optionally also mint a token from an ordinary app (right audience, NO enforcement claim) to prove the claim check is real.\n2. **Rejections** — `initialize` with no bearer → 401; garbage bearer → 401; valid JWT without the `gatewayEnforcement` claim → 401 naming the claim. This is the gate that keeps direct callers (task-agent bypassing the gateway) out of the tool list entirely.\n3. **Success** — `initialize` → `notifications/initialized` → `tools/list` carrying the enforcement JWT: the full MCP session handshake passes the gate.\n4. **Escape hatch** — the same request with `MCP_GATEWAY_ENFORCEMENT=false` on the server: the unauthenticated call goes through again.\n\n**Prerequisites**\n- mcp-todos-server running on :9000 **with enforcement on**. The repo's live `.env` ships `MCP_GATEWAY_ENFORCEMENT=false` (gateway-less dev default) — flip it to `true`, or run:\n  `cd mcp-todos-server && MCP_GATEWAY_ENFORCEMENT=true uv run uvicorn app.main:app --port 9000`\n- PingOne app #7 created per README \"PingOne setup\" (Client Credentials; scope on the SAME resource as app #6 so `aud` = `http://localhost:9000/mcp`; custom claim `gatewayEnforcement` = boolean `true`). Fill in `enforcement_client_id` / `enforcement_client_secret` collection variables.\n- Optional (for the wrong-claim 401): credentials of any ordinary client whose scope is on that same resource — app #6's (`TODOS_MCP_CLIENT_ID`) work. Fill `ordinary_client_id` / `ordinary_client_secret`.\n\n**Run order**: run folder 0 first (it sets the token variables), then 1, then 2. Or use Postman Runner over the whole collection in order.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "variable": [
